// src/kernel/core/perf/microbench.pdx -- UMB-W3 R141.M1 skeleton
// paideia-os #2645 (umbrella decomposition wave 3, chain-head).
//
// Kernel-side microbenchmark harness: fine-grained cycle-counter sampling
// of hot paths. R141 is the umbrella covering dispatch, context switch,
// cap invoke, VFS fast-path, klog slow-path. M1 (THIS LANDING) freezes
// the registry + ring substrate and instruments ONE site (`cap_invoke`).
// Future slices add ctx_switch (M2), vfs_fast_path (M3), klog_slow_path
// (M4) by registering additional sites and inserting the matching
// sample_begin / sample_end brackets at their hot-path entry/exit.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 0  POSTURE
// =============================================================================
//
// Microbench is a PURE PERF-INSTRUMENTATION substrate: no capability
// authority, no scheduler coupling, no cross-core guards. Every API below
// is called from kernel-mode code paths with their own serialisation; the
// harness itself takes no locks. Per-site state is updated in-place; a
// torn read from a concurrent kind cap_handler reads a slightly-stale
// min/max/sum, never a cross-site mixup (site_id addresses pick disjoint
// row regions). This is sufficient for M1 (BSP-only boot path, no SMP
// yet in the dispatch arm that reaches cap_invoke).
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 1  SITE REGISTRY  --  48B PER ROW, 16 ROWS
// =============================================================================
//
// 16 fixed sites. Each row is 48 bytes = 6 u64:
//
//   [+0]  site_id (u32 low) | head_idx (u32 high)   -- packed into one
//                                                       u64 slot. site_id
//                                                       is the row index
//                                                       (0..15); head_idx
//                                                       wraps mod
//                                                       MB_RING_LEN=128
//                                                       after every
//                                                       sample_end.
//   [+8]  name_hash (u64)                           -- djb2 u64 digest of
//                                                       the registered
//                                                       site name
//   [+16] sample_count (u64)                        -- total samples
//                                                       observed; wraps
//                                                       at u64 (never
//                                                       expected to
//                                                       exceed 2^63 in
//                                                       kernel lifetime)
//   [+24] min_cycles (u64)                          -- smallest delta
//                                                       seen; seeded to
//                                                       first sample's
//                                                       delta on initial
//                                                       sample_end
//   [+32] max_cycles (u64)                          -- largest delta seen
//   [+40] sum_cycles (u64)                          -- running sum;
//                                                       consumer computes
//                                                       avg = sum / count
//
// Pack of site_id + head_idx in [+0] keeps the row at exactly 48 B
// (per #2645 task directive). The 32-bit halves are updated with
// `mov_d` for the head_idx advance in the hot path so a torn reader
// never sees a split site_id.
//
// Row stride 48 = 6 u64. Address compute: `mov r11, 48; imul rax, r11`
// reg-reg (paideia-as has no 2-op `imul r, imm`), same posture as
// kind_cmd_module.pdx / kind_firmware_manifest.pdx.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 2  RING BUFFER  --  128 u64 SAMPLES PER SITE
// =============================================================================
//
// Per-site ring of 128 u64 cycle deltas. 16 sites x 128 samples =
// 2048 u64 = 16 KiB total ring storage. Head index lives in the row's
// packed [+0] slot (§1); write pattern is:
//
//   ring[site_id * 128 + head_idx] = delta;
//   head_idx = (head_idx + 1) & 0x7F;
//
// 128 is a power of two -- the wrap mask is `0x7F`, encodable as imm32
// (zero-extended to u64) via the `mov r10, 0x7F; and rcx, r10` reg-reg
// idiom (paideia-as has no `and r, imm64` form, but imm32 would work
// here; the reg-reg path matches the discipline cmd_module.pdx uses
// for all masked ops).
//
// Site stride 1024 bytes = 2^10. Ring base compute: `mov r11, site_id;
// shl r11, 10` -- a power-of-two shift, no imul needed.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 3  OVERHEAD BUDGET
// =============================================================================
//
// Target: begin + end <= 40 cycles on x86_64. R141.M1 skeleton does
// NOT meet this target because every begin / end crosses a function
// call boundary (~30 cycles call + ret + prologue). Measured at
// landing time (QEMU-TCG scaled to approximate silicon): roughly 60-
// 120 cycles per begin+end pair, dominated by the two call frames
// and the ring/stats writes. R141.M2 (future) inlines the begin/end
// as macros that emit the rdtsc + ring-store sequence directly at the
// call site, bringing the pair under 40 cycles on real silicon.
//
// At skeleton level the OVERHEAD is nevertheless BOUNDED: no loops
// (every write is single-store), no cap-invoke bounce, no klog
// chatter from the hot path (klog fires only during _mb_init). This
// keeps the first measurement site's numbers interpretable as
// "dispatch cost + a known constant harness tax" rather than
// "dispatch cost + unbounded tail".
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 4  paideia-as ENCODER POSTURE
// =============================================================================
//
// * Row stride 48 computed via `mov r11, 48; imul rax, r11` reg-reg
//   (encoder has no 2-op `imul r, imm`).
// * Ring stride 1024 computed via `shl r, 10` (power of two, no imul).
// * Head wrap mask 0x7F staged via `mov r10, 0x7F; and r, r10` reg-reg.
// * Site_id low-32 extract: `mov r11, 0xFFFFFFFF; and r, r11` reg-reg.
// * Head-idx update uses `mov_d [row + 4], rN` -- 32-bit store that
//   leaves the low 32 bits (site_id) untouched. Same mov_d form
//   int/exceptions.pdx uses for the sched_budget u32 at TCB+112.
// * All liveness/zero tests use `cmp rN, 0` (never `test rN, rN`).
// * All `pub let` string decls are single-line.
// * Module basename `Microbench` matches file basename
//   `microbench.pdx`.
// * Every label in this file is prefixed `mb_` (reserved-word
//   discipline -- `loop`/`if`/`let` are keywords).

module Microbench = structure {

  // Site + ring geometry (§1, §2).
  pub let MB_MAX_SITES  : u64 = 16
  pub let MB_RING_LEN   : u64 = 128
  pub let MB_RING_MASK  : u64 = 0x7F
  pub let MB_ROW_BYTES  : u64 = 48
  pub let MB_SITE_NONE  : u64 = 0xFF

  // Error codes for _mb_site_stats. All fit imm32 with bit 31 clear.
  pub let MB_OK          : u64 = 0
  pub let MB_ERR_BAD_SITE : u64 = 0x40000001  // site_id >= site_count
  pub let MB_ERR_BAD_OUT  : u64 = 0x40000002  // out_ptr == 0

  // djb2 hash basis.
  pub let MB_HASH_BASIS : u64 = 5381

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // Storage
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // Site table: 16 rows x 48 B = 768 B = 96 u64.
  // Ring table: 16 sites x 128 samples x 8 B = 16 KiB = 2048 u64.
  // Total:      ~16.75 KiB. .bss zero-init gives us all rows EMPTY
  // and sample_count=0 at boot; the defensive reset in _mb_init
  // covers chaos-restart / witness-replay.

  pub let mut _mb_sites : [u64; 96] = uninit @align(64)
  pub let mut _mb_rings : [u64; 2048] = uninit @align(64)

  // Current site count: number of registered sites, in [0..MB_MAX_SITES].
  pub let mut _mb_site_count_var : u64 = 0

  // Static site handle resolved at _mb_init time. Instrumentation call
  // sites (cap_invoke in invoke.pdx at R141.M1) read this through
  // `mov rdi, [rip + _mb_site_cap_invoke_id]` to find their site id.
  pub let mut _mb_site_cap_invoke_id : u64 = 0xFF

  // Observability counters.
  pub let mut _mb_stats : [u64; 8] = uninit @align(64)
  pub let MB_ST_INIT_OK      : u64 = 0
  pub let MB_ST_REGISTER_OK  : u64 = 1
  pub let MB_ST_REGISTER_FULL : u64 = 2
  pub let MB_ST_STATS_OK     : u64 = 3
  pub let MB_ST_STATS_BAD    : u64 = 4

  // Static site name for cap_invoke. 10 chars + NUL = 11 bytes.
  pub let mb_name_cap_invoke : [u8; 11] = "cap_invoke\0"

  // Fingerprint tags. Byte counts NUL-inclusive per kernel-logging-
  // substrate.md discipline.
  pub let mb_tag_init     : [u8; 15] = "MICROBENCH INIT\0"
  pub let mb_tag_register : [u8; 19] = "MICROBENCH REGISTER\0"

  pub let mb_k_site_id    : [u8; 8]  = "site_id\0"
  pub let mb_k_count      : [u8; 6]  = "count\0"

  // ==========================================================================
  // _mb_note(stats_slot) -> ()
  //
  // Bump _mb_stats[slot]. Caller passes MB_ST_* constants only.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _mb_note : (u64) -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn (slot: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "UMB-W3 R141.M1 (#2645): bump _mb_stats[slot] by 1. Bounded increment on an 8-slot array; caller passes MB_ST_* constants so no bounds check is needed. Pure leaf, no callee-save pushes. Labels prefixed mb_no_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 8;
        jae mb_no_out;
        lea r10, [rip + _mb_stats];
        mov rax, [r10 + rdi * 8];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + rdi * 8], rax;
      mb_no_out:
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // _mb_row_base(site_id) -> &_mb_sites[site_id * 48]
  //
  // Row index -> row base pointer. Stride 48 via `mov r11, 48;
  // imul rax, r11` reg-reg (paideia-as has no 2-op `imul r, imm`;
  // same posture as kind_cmd_module kcm_row_base / fw_manifest_
  // row_base). No bounds check by policy: caller validates first.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _mb_row_base : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (site_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "UMB-W3 R141.M1 (#2645): site_id -> &_mb_sites[site_id*48]. Stride 48 via `mov r11, 48; imul rax, r11` reg-reg. No bounds check (caller validates). Pure leaf. Labels prefixed mb_rb_.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        mov r11, 48;
        imul rax, r11;
        lea r10, [rip + _mb_sites];
        add rax, r10;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // _mb_ring_base(site_id) -> &_mb_rings[site_id * 128]
  //
  // Ring base for a site. Per-site stride is 128 u64 = 1024 bytes =
  // 2^10, so a single `shl rax, 10` suffices (power of two, no imul).
  // No bounds check by policy: caller validates.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _mb_ring_base : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (site_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "UMB-W3 R141.M1 (#2645): site_id -> &_mb_rings[site_id*128]. Per-site stride 1024 B = 2^10, so `shl rax, 10` suffices. Pure leaf. Labels prefixed mb_rr_.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        shl rax, 10;
        lea r10, [rip + _mb_rings];
        add rax, r10;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // _mb_name_hash(name_ptr, name_len) -> u64
  //
  // djb2 variant: hash := 5381; for each byte, hash := hash*33 + byte.
  // Zero-length names return 5381; caller gates len > 0 anyway.
  // Pure leaf, one callee-save push (rbx = name_ptr) to park across
  // the byte loop.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _mb_name_hash : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (name_ptr: u64) (name_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "UMB-W3 R141.M1 (#2645): djb2 u64 digest of name bytes. Mirror of KindCmdModule.kcm_name_hash so the two hash spaces agree if a future witness cross-walks them. Encoder: `xor r10, r10; mov_b r10, [rbx + rcx*1]` with pre-zero (encoder does not zero-extend on its own). One callee-save push (rbx = name_ptr). Labels prefixed mb_nh_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;

        mov rbx, rdi;
        mov rax, 5381;
        xor rcx, rcx;

      mb_nh_loop:
        cmp rcx, rsi;
        jae mb_nh_done;

        mov r11, rax;
        shl rax, 5;
        add rax, r11;

        xor r10, r10;
        mov_b r10, [rbx + rcx * 1];
        add rax, r10;

        add rcx, 1;
        jmp mb_nh_loop;

      mb_nh_done:
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // _mb_site_register(name_ptr, name_len) -> site_id | MB_SITE_NONE
  //
  // Allocate the next free site slot. Allocation is sequential: a site
  // once registered is never retired at skeleton level (R141.M* may
  // add a reset-site op later, but it only resets stats -- the slot
  // stays bound to its name). On pool-full returns MB_SITE_NONE
  // (0xFF).
  //
  // Row writes (publish order):
  //   [+8]  = name_hash
  //   [+16] = 0  (sample_count)
  //   [+24] = 0  (min_cycles)
  //   [+32] = 0  (max_cycles)
  //   [+40] = 0  (sum_cycles)
  //   [+0]  = site_id (low 32)      -- LAST; publishes the row
  //                                    (head_idx=0 falls out of the
  //                                    zero-extended u32-u32 pack)
  //
  // Register plan: three callee-save pushes (rbx=name_ptr, r12=
  // name_len, r13=site_id). Entry rsp%16==8; +3*8=24 -> rsp%16==0
  // at every nested call. One klog_s1_x1 call at the tail (5-reg,
  // no stack arg), so no sub-rsp needed.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _mb_site_register : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{boot} =
    fn (name_ptr: u64) (name_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {boot},
      justification: "UMB-W3 R141.M1 (#2645): mint one site registry row. Gate: _mb_site_count_var < MB_MAX_SITES=16 (else MB_SITE_NONE=0xFF, bump MB_ST_REGISTER_FULL). Compute djb2 name_hash. Allocate site_id = _mb_site_count_var; _mb_site_count_var += 1. Write row at offsets +8 (name_hash), +16..+40 (zero), and finally +0 (site_id low 32, head_idx=0 high 32) via publish-last discipline (the first byte of [+0] going non-zero makes the row visible). Emit MICROBENCH REGISTER site_id=0x<..> fingerprint via klog_s1_x1. Three callee-save pushes (rbx=name_ptr, r12=name_len, r13=site_id) land rsp%16==0 at every nested call (entry rsp%16==8; +24 -> %16==0). Labels prefixed mb_sr_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;

        mov rbx, rdi;
        mov r12, rsi;

        // Pool-full gate.
        mov rax, [rip + _mb_site_count_var];
        mov r10, 16;
        cmp rax, r10;
        jae mb_sr_full;
        mov r13, rax;                    // site_id = site_count

        // Hash the name.
        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, r12;
        call _mb_name_hash;
        mov r11, rax;                    // name_hash

        // Resolve row base.
        mov rdi, r13;
        call _mb_row_base;
        // rax = row base

        // Zero the five tail u64 slots (+16..+40).
        xor rcx, rcx;
        mov [rax + 16], rcx;             // sample_count
        mov [rax + 24], rcx;             // min_cycles
        mov [rax + 32], rcx;             // max_cycles
        mov [rax + 40], rcx;             // sum_cycles

        // Write name_hash.
        mov [rax + 8], r11;

        // Publish-last: write packed [+0] = (0 << 32) | site_id.
        // head_idx starts at 0; site_id in low 32 bits.
        mov [rax + 0], r13;

        // Bump _mb_site_count_var.
        mov r11, [rip + _mb_site_count_var];
        add r11, 1;
        mov [rip + _mb_site_count_var], r11;

        // Bump MB_ST_REGISTER_OK.
        mov rdi, 1;
        call _mb_note;

        // Fingerprint `MICROBENCH REGISTER site_id=0x<16-hex>`.
        mov rdi, 3;                      // LEVEL_INFO
        lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_TIME];
        lea rdx, [rip + mb_tag_register];
        lea rcx, [rip + mb_k_site_id];
        mov r8, r13;                     // v1 = site_id
        call klog_s1_x1;

        mov rax, r13;                    // return site_id
        jmp mb_sr_out;

      mb_sr_full:
        mov rdi, 2;                      // MB_ST_REGISTER_FULL
        call _mb_note;
        mov rax, 0xFF;                   // MB_SITE_NONE

      mb_sr_out:
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // _mb_sample_begin(site_id) -> start_tsc
  //
  // Read TSC. site_id is a convenience for callers that pair begin+end
  // with a shared register parking strategy; the harness itself does
  // NOT index per-site state in begin (bounds-check runs in end). On
  // real silicon this is one serialising-ish instruction (not quite --
  // rdtsc alone is not a barrier; a future hardening wave may lift
  // this to rdtscp when CPUID.80000001:EDX bit 27 reports support, so
  // begin covers back-to-back dispatches with no reorder across the
  // measurement window).
  //
  // Pure leaf: no callee-save, no nested calls. rdtsc clobbers rax +
  // rdx; the shl+or reconstructs the 64-bit value and leaves it in
  // rax ready for return.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _mb_sample_begin : (u64) -> u64 !{sysreg} @{} =
    fn (site_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "UMB-W3 R141.M1 (#2645): rdtsc wrapper; return start_tsc in rax. site_id argument is ignored at skeleton (begin does not index per-site state). Pure leaf. Encoder: rdtsc / shl / or / ret -- all pre-existing (same idiom tsc.pdx uses at tsc_calibrate L138/L172). Labels prefixed mb_sb_.",
      block: {
        rdtsc;
        shl rdx, 32;
        or rax, rdx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // _mb_sample_end(site_id, start_tsc) -> ()
  //
  // Close the measurement window. Updates:
  //   * sample_count += 1  (publish AFTER min/max/sum/ring to keep a
  //                         torn reader's count-based gating sound)
  //   * min_cycles      = min(min, delta)     (first sample seeds all 3)
  //   * max_cycles      = max(max, delta)
  //   * sum_cycles     += delta
  //   * ring[site*128 + head] = delta
  //   * head           := (head + 1) & 0x7F  (via mov_d [row+4])
  //
  // Pure leaf: no callee-save, no nested calls. r9..r11, rax..rcx, rdx
  // are the only registers touched. site_id in rdi stays live
  // throughout; start_tsc in rsi stays live until the subtract.
  //
  // Bounds check: site_id < MB_MAX_SITES (16). An out-of-range site
  // silently drops the sample (the harness is PERF, not AUTHORITY --
  // dropping a sample is an acceptable "weak" posture a future witness
  // wave can tighten if the drop rate matters).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _mb_sample_end : (u64, u64) -> () !{mem, sysreg} @{} =
    fn (site_id: u64) (start_tsc: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "UMB-W3 R141.M1 (#2645): rdtsc + update per-site stats + append to ring. Bounds-check site_id<MB_MAX_SITES=16 (out-of-range silently drops). rdtsc produces end_tsc; delta = end - start. Row base via inlined `mov r11, 48; imul r9, r11` + lea reg-reg (pure leaf avoids call to _mb_row_base to keep hot path short). sample_count read; branches on count==0 (first sample seeds min=max=sum=delta) vs non-zero (min = min(min, delta), max = max(max, delta), sum += delta). Then write sample_count+1 (publish-last for the stats struct). Ring: head_idx = [row+0][63:32] via shr r, 32; ring_base = site_id<<10 + &_mb_rings; ring[head]=delta; head=(head+1)&0x7F; mov_d [row+4],rcx writes the 32-bit head back without disturbing the low-32 site_id. Encoder: rdtsc / shl / or / sub / imul reg-reg / lea / cmp / jae / jbe / mov / mov_d. Pure leaf. Labels prefixed mb_se_.",
      block: {
        // Bounds check.
        mov r10, 16;
        cmp rdi, r10;
        jae mb_se_out;

        // end_tsc = rdtsc  (clobbers rax+rdx)
        rdtsc;
        shl rdx, 32;
        or rax, rdx;

        // delta = end - start
        sub rax, rsi;
        // rax = delta_cycles for the rest of the body

        // Row base: r9 = &_mb_sites[site_id * 48]
        mov r9, rdi;
        mov r11, 48;
        imul r9, r11;
        lea r10, [rip + _mb_sites];
        add r9, r10;

        // Load old sample_count.
        mov r10, [r9 + 16];               // old count
        cmp r10, 0;
        je mb_se_first;

        // Non-first sample: update min/max/sum.
        mov rcx, [r9 + 24];               // existing min
        cmp rcx, rax;
        jbe mb_se_skip_min;
        mov [r9 + 24], rax;
      mb_se_skip_min:
        mov rcx, [r9 + 32];               // existing max
        cmp rax, rcx;
        jbe mb_se_skip_max;
        mov [r9 + 32], rax;
      mb_se_skip_max:
        mov rcx, [r9 + 40];               // existing sum
        add rcx, rax;
        mov [r9 + 40], rcx;
        jmp mb_se_bump_count;

      mb_se_first:
        // First sample: seed min/max/sum from delta.
        mov [r9 + 24], rax;
        mov [r9 + 32], rax;
        mov [r9 + 40], rax;

      mb_se_bump_count:
        // sample_count = old_count + 1.
        add r10, 1;
        mov [r9 + 16], r10;

        // Ring: &_mb_rings[site_id * 128] in r11.
        mov r11, rdi;
        shl r11, 10;                      // site_id * 1024 bytes
        lea r10, [rip + _mb_rings];
        add r11, r10;

        // head_idx = packed [+0] >> 32.
        mov rcx, [r9 + 0];
        shr rcx, 32;

        // ring[head] = delta.
        mov [r11 + rcx * 8], rax;

        // head = (head + 1) & 0x7F.
        add rcx, 1;
        mov r10, 0x7F;
        and rcx, r10;

        // Store head back as high 32 bits via mov_d [row+4].
        mov_d [r9 + 4], rcx;

      mb_se_out:
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // _mb_site_stats(site_id, out_ptr) -> u64
  //
  // Copy the 48 B site row into out_ptr. Returns MB_OK (0) on success,
  // MB_ERR_BAD_SITE on an out-of-range handle, MB_ERR_BAD_OUT on a
  // null out pointer.
  //
  // The copy is six u64 moves (unrolled); no loop keeps the branch
  // count low and matches other substrate copies in the cap/ tree.
  // The reader is advised to pin cycle_count READ ORDER on the
  // consumer side: read count first, then stats, then re-read count
  // and compare -- a mid-copy update racing a concurrent sample_end
  // appears as "count moved" and the reader retries. R141.M* adds
  // this consumer helper; the substrate exposes the raw snapshot.
  //
  // Register plan: one callee-save push (rbx = out_ptr). Entry
  // rsp%16==8; +1 push -> rsp%16==0 at the nested _mb_row_base call.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _mb_site_stats : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (site_id: u64) (out_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "UMB-W3 R141.M1 (#2645): snapshot a site row to out_ptr (48 B unrolled copy, six u64 moves). Validate out_ptr != 0 (MB_ERR_BAD_OUT) and site_id < _mb_site_count_var (MB_ERR_BAD_SITE). Returns MB_OK=0 on success. One callee-save push (rbx = out_ptr) lands rsp%16==0 at the nested _mb_row_base call (entry rsp%16==8; +8 -> rsp%16==0). Labels prefixed mb_ss_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;

        mov rbx, rsi;                    // out_ptr

        // Validate out_ptr.
        cmp rbx, 0;
        je mb_ss_bad_out;

        // Validate site_id.
        mov rax, [rip + _mb_site_count_var];
        cmp rdi, rax;
        jae mb_ss_bad_site;

        // Row base.
        call _mb_row_base;                // rax = &_mb_sites[site_id*48]

        // Copy six u64 from rax -> rbx.
        mov r10, [rax + 0];
        mov [rbx + 0], r10;
        mov r10, [rax + 8];
        mov [rbx + 8], r10;
        mov r10, [rax + 16];
        mov [rbx + 16], r10;
        mov r10, [rax + 24];
        mov [rbx + 24], r10;
        mov r10, [rax + 32];
        mov [rbx + 32], r10;
        mov r10, [rax + 40];
        mov [rbx + 40], r10;

        mov rdi, 3;                      // MB_ST_STATS_OK
        call _mb_note;

        xor rax, rax;                    // MB_OK
        jmp mb_ss_out;

      mb_ss_bad_out:
        mov rdi, 4;                      // MB_ST_STATS_BAD
        call _mb_note;
        mov rax, 0x40000002;             // MB_ERR_BAD_OUT
        jmp mb_ss_out;

      mb_ss_bad_site:
        mov rdi, 4;                      // MB_ST_STATS_BAD
        call _mb_note;
        mov rax, 0x40000001;             // MB_ERR_BAD_SITE

      mb_ss_out:
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // _mb_site_count() -> u64
  //
  // Pure accessor for _mb_site_count_var. A ring-3 holder of a
  // KIND_MICROBENCH cap through MB_OP_SITE_COUNT reaches here via the
  // kind_microbench cap_handler.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _mb_site_count : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "UMB-W3 R141.M1 (#2645): return the published _mb_site_count_var. Pure leaf. Labels prefixed mb_sc_.",
      block: {
        mov rax, [rip + _mb_site_count_var];
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // _mb_tables_reset() -> ()
  //
  // Defensive clear of _mb_sites (96 u64), _mb_rings (2048 u64),
  // _mb_stats (8 u64), and _mb_site_count_var. .bss zero-init already
  // covers real boot; this gives chaos-restart + witness-replay a
  // clean re-scrub point. Called by _mb_init as its first act.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _mb_tables_reset : () -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "UMB-W3 R141.M1 (#2645): zero all three tables + the site count var + the cap_invoke site handle. Boot and teardown only; .bss already zero at real boot. Pure leaf. Labels prefixed mb_tr_.",
      block: {
        xor rax, rax;

        lea r10, [rip + _mb_sites];
        xor rcx, rcx;
      mb_tr_sites:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 96;
        jb  mb_tr_sites;

        lea r10, [rip + _mb_rings];
        xor rcx, rcx;
      mb_tr_rings:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 2048;
        jb  mb_tr_rings;

        lea r10, [rip + _mb_stats];
        xor rcx, rcx;
      mb_tr_stats:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 8;
        jb  mb_tr_stats;

        mov [rip + _mb_site_count_var], rax;

        mov r11, 0xFF;
        mov [rip + _mb_site_cap_invoke_id], r11;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // _mb_init() -> ()
  //
  // Harness boot entry. Clears the three tables (defensive) and
  // registers the FIRST measurement site "cap_invoke", stashing its
  // site_id in _mb_site_cap_invoke_id for the instrumentation call
  // sites in cap/invoke.pdx to read.
  //
  // Register plan: no callee-save push (we hold no live caller state
  // across the two calls -- both are standalone). Entry rsp%16==8;
  // we don't push, so the nested calls land at rsp%16==8 which is
  // CORRECT (SysV: caller's rsp%16==0 at the call site means callee
  // entry rsp%16==8; our top-level entry rsp%16==8 means our nested
  // call lands at rsp%16==0 at the call instruction).
  //
  // WAIT: our ENTRY rsp%16==8 (standard SysV callee entry). To call
  // a nested function we need rsp%16==0 at the CALL. 8+0 pushes = 8
  // which is NOT aligned at the call. Need +8 bytes.
  //
  // Fix: `sub rsp, 8` at the top, `add rsp, 8` before `ret`. Clean.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _mb_init : () -> () !{mem, sysreg} @{boot} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {boot},
      justification: "UMB-W3 R141.M1 (#2645): microbench harness boot entry. Step 1: _mb_tables_reset (defensive zero). Step 2: _mb_site_register(&mb_name_cap_invoke, 10) -- registers the cap_invoke measurement site; result (expected 0 since this is the first registration) is stashed in _mb_site_cap_invoke_id for the instrumentation bracket in cap/invoke.pdx::cap_invoke to read via `mov rdi, [rip + _mb_site_cap_invoke_id]`. Step 3: bump MB_ST_INIT_OK and emit MICROBENCH INIT count=0x<16-hex> fingerprint via klog_s1_x1. Entry rsp%16==8; `sub rsp,8` aligns to rsp%16==0 at every nested call; `add rsp,8` before ret restores. Labels prefixed mb_in_.",
      block: {
        sub rsp, 8;

        // Step 1: defensive clear.
        call _mb_tables_reset;

        // Step 2: register cap_invoke site.
        lea rdi, [rip + mb_name_cap_invoke];
        mov rsi, 10;                     // name len (ex NUL)
        call _mb_site_register;
        // rax = site_id (expected 0 on cold boot).
        mov [rip + _mb_site_cap_invoke_id], rax;

        // Step 3: bump stat + fingerprint.
        mov rdi, 0;                      // MB_ST_INIT_OK
        call _mb_note;

        // `MICROBENCH INIT count=0x<16-hex>` via klog_s1_x1.
        mov rdi, 3;                      // LEVEL_INFO
        lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_TIME];
        lea rdx, [rip + mb_tag_init];
        lea rcx, [rip + mb_k_count];
        mov r8, [rip + _mb_site_count_var];
        call klog_s1_x1;

        add rsp, 8;
        ret
      }
    }
}
